1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Here's a problem every growing Cloud integration hits.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:05,000
The same model name,

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,000
the same prompt text,

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
and the same retry logic end up

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
copied across dozens of files.

6
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Then a single change means editing all of them.

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This lesson covers refactoring that out,

8
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
isolating calls, extracting

9
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
shared prompts, and reducing coupling.

10
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's define refactoring cleanly

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
because people use the word loosely.

12
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Refactoring is restructuring working code

13
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
to make it more readable and maintainable

14
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
without changing what it does.

15
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Three ideas anchor it.

16
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
First, it's behavior preserving.

17
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
The output is the same before and after.

18
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
You've only reshaped how the code is organized.

19
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Nobody using the system should notice a thing.

20
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Second, tests are your safety net.

21
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,000
They confirm behavior didn't change

22
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
so you can restructure with confidence.

23
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Third, keep it separate.

24
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
A refactor and a feature change

25
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
shouldn't share a commit

26
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
because mixing them makes review and rollback painful.

27
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Behavior preserving, test backed, and kept separate.

28
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
That's a disciplined refactor.

29
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The highest value refactor in most Cloud integrations

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
is isolating the API call behind one clean interface.

31
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Instead of scattering raw SDK calls all over the code base,

32
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
you wrap them in a single client module

33
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
that the rest of your app calls.

34
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The model name, the headers, the retry policy,

35
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
and the error handling all live in that one place.

36
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Everything else depends on your interface,

37
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
not on the raw API.

38
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The payoff shows up the moment you need to change something.

39
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Swapping to a new model, adjusting a header,

40
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
or tweaking retry behavior becomes a one module edit

41
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
instead of a hunt across the whole project.

42
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
One clean seam is worth a lot.

43
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Let's contrast the two states directly

44
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
because the difference is what sells the refactor.

45
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
In the scattered version, the model name, the headers,

46
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
and the retry logic are copy pasted across many files.

47
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
When you need to pin a new model,

48
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
you're editing every one of them and you will miss one.

49
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
That missed spot is a bug waiting to happen.

50
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,000
In the consolidated version,

51
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
every call roots through a single shared client.

52
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
A model pin, a header change,

53
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
or a retry adjustment happens exactly once

54
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
and every caller picks it up automatically.

55
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Same behavior either way,

56
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
but one of them makes change cheap

57
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,000
and the other makes it dangerous.

58
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
That's the whole argument for the seam.

59
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Prompts have the same duplication problem as code

60
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:38,000
and the same fix.

61
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
When the same block of instructions

62
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
shows up in several call sites,

63
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
that's duplication waiting to drift.

64
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
One copy gets updated, the others don't,

65
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
and now your behavior is inconsistent

66
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
depending on which path ran.

67
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The refactor is to pull that repeated instruction text

68
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
into one shared named fragment

69
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
and store reusable system prompts

70
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
as units you can test on their own.

71
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Then each call site references the shared fragment

72
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
instead of carrying its own copy.

73
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Fix the wording once and every caller

74
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
gets the corrected version at the same time.

75
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Treat prompt text as a reusable asset,

76
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
not as string literals scattered around.

77
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
When I go looking for refactoring work

78
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:23,000
in a cloud integration,

79
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
four targets come up again and again.

80
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
First, duplicated prompts.

81
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
The same instruction text scattered across files

82
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
which you extract into one shared fragment.

83
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Second, inline config, model names,

84
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
and settings hard-coded at each call site

85
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
which belong in one config module.

86
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Third, repeated call logic, the same request,

87
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
retry, and parse code copied everywhere

88
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
which you wrap behind a single client.

89
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
And fourth, tangled concerns where prompt building,

90
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
the API call, and parsing all live in one function

91
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
which you split apart.

92
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Duplicated prompts, inline config,

93
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
repeated call logic, tangled concerns.

94
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Those four cover most of it.

95
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
All of this rolls up into one principle,

96
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
reducing coupling.

97
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Coupling is how much a change in one place

98
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
forces changes somewhere else.

99
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Tight coupling is why a simple model pin

100
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
turns into a 20-file edit.

101
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
When you isolate calls behind a client

102
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
and extract prompts into shared fragments,

103
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
you make change local.

104
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
A model pin update lives in the client.

105
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
A prompt version bump changes one shared fragment.

106
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:38,000
A config tweak flows out from a single source

107
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
because the change touches one place instead of many.

108
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Swapping a model or rolling a prompt version

109
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,000
becomes cheap and low risk.

110
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Low coupling is what lets you move fast

111
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
without breaking the integration every time.

112
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Refactoring comes in two sizes

113
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
and both belong in your workflow.

114
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Small refactors are the everyday kind.

115
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Rename a confusing variable, extract a helper,

116
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
delete a duplicated block.

117
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
They're low risk and you do them continuously

118
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:08,000
as you touch the code.

119
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Large refactors reshape bigger structures

120
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
like redrawing module boundaries

121
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
or introducing that client layer we talked about.

122
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
They carry more risk so you lean hard on tests

123
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
and move in small, reviewable steps

124
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
rather than one giant rewrite.

125
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
The rule is the same at both sizes.

126
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
You preserve behavior and you keep the refactor

127
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
out of any commit that also changes a feature.

128
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Small or large, disciplined refactoring

129
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
never mixes with new behavior.

130
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Just like with review,

131
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Clod is a strong refactoring partner

132
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
and the same boundary applies.

133
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
It's genuinely good at this.

134
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
It can spot duplication you've stopped noticing

135
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
and suggest a clean extraction.

136
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
It can look at scattered call sites

137
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
and propose a client interface to pull them behind.

138
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
It can draft the mechanical parts

139
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
of a large refactor quickly.

140
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
But two things keep you safe.

141
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Tests still verify that behavior stayed exactly the same

142
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
because a confident refactor suggestion

143
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
can still change output in a subtle way

144
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
and you review each step and approve it before it lands.

145
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Clod accelerates the work.

146
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
It doesn't replace the tests or the human sign-off

147
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
that make refactoring trustworthy.

148
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Here's the bottom line.

149
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Refactoring keeps a Clod integration easy to change

150
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
without changing what it does.

151
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Isolate your API calls behind one clean client.

152
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Extract shared prompts and config into reusable units

153
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
and reduce coupling so a model or prompt change

154
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
touches a single place.

155
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Just remember, tests preserve behavior

156
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
and a human still approves every step.

157
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
That covers refactoring prompts and application code.

158
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
I'll see you in the next lesson.

